{"name":"Willow Wind","hex":"#d5dca9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"willow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous trees or shrubs in the genus Salix, in the willow family Salicaceae, found primarily on moist soils in cooler zones in the northern hemisphere.","The wood of these trees."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open and cleanse (cotton, flax, wool, etc.) by means of a willow.","To form a shape or move in a way similar to the long, slender branches of a willow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/willow"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Willow","blocks":[{"text":"Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Salix_alba_Morton.jpg/330px-Salix_alba_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At the base of the petiole, a pair of stipules form. These may fall in spring, or last for much of the summer or even for more than one year (marcescence).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalix_silesiaca_a5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young male catkin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung-catkin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/1280px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co